## `.cursorrules`

```markdown
Project-wide instructions live in `.cursorrules`. Task-specific guidance lives in `.cursor/skills/design-system/SKILL.md` for UI and Figma work and `.cursor/skills/backend-reliability/SKILL.md` for backend and API work. The always-on change discipline rule lives in `.cursor/rules/change-discipline.mdc`.

## Change discipline

- Follow the nearest existing module, page, or component.
- Keep changes limited to the requested task.
- Add dependencies, abstractions, and refactors only when the task requires them.
- If an existing pattern conflicts with the requested behavior or authorization requirements, explain the conflict before copying it.

## Frontend

- Use existing components from `@repo/theme`.
- Read `packages/theme/src/components/index.ts` and `packages/theme/src/components/ThemeProvider/ThemeProvider.scss` before adding components or colors.
- Use existing semantic CSS variables for colors and typography.
- Define spacing, border radius, and shadows in component SCSS modules where the theme has no corresponding tokens.
- Follow `.cursor/skills/design-system/SKILL.md` for UI and Figma work.

## Backend

- Follow the nearest module under `apps/backend/src/modules/`.
- Database models live in `packages/common/src/models/`.
- After a backend GraphQL schema change, run `npm run generate` from `apps/backend`.
- Do not hand-edit `generated-types/`, `generated-typedefs/`, or `generated/`; update their sources and use the generator.
- Keep the persisted-operation gateway at `/api/graphql`.

## Authentication and authorization

- `@auth` checks whether the caller is authenticated. It does not check record ownership or staff permissions.
- Apply `@auth` to fields and mutations that require a signed-in user.
- User-owned operations also require controller-level authorization. Scope record access to the caller using the existing ownership predicate, such as `createdBy: context.user._id`.
- Never authorize a user-owned write using authentication and a caller-supplied record ID alone.
- Protect private user-owned reads with the corresponding ownership or access check.
- Staff-only operations require the existing staff authorization check.
- Public catalog reads stay public, matching the nearest existing field.

## GraphQL errors

- Use `GraphQLError` and preserve the error semantics of the nearest relevant controller.
- Existing codes include `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `FORBIDDEN`, `BAD_USER_INPUT`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `NOT_FOUND`, `CONFLICT`, and `INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR`.
- Preserve `CONFLICT` for conflict cases and `INVALID_ARGUMENT` where the existing API uses it; do not replace them with `BAD_USER_INPUT` merely to shorten this list.
- Return expected failures as GraphQL errors. Let unexpected failures surface; do not hide them in empty `catch` blocks.
- Follow `.cursor/skills/backend-reliability/SKILL.md` for backend and API work.
```

## `.cursor/skills/design-system/SKILL.md`

```markdown
---
name: design-system
description: >-
  Build Berkeleytime UI from the @repo/theme design system and ThemeProvider
  tokens. Use when creating or editing a page, component, prototype, layout,
  SCSS module, or when the user mentions Figma, design, mockup, tokens, or
  styling.
---

# Berkeleytime design system

Build screens from components and tokens that already exist. Read
`packages/theme/src/components/index.ts` and
`packages/theme/src/components/ThemeProvider/ThemeProvider.scss` before
adding a component or a color. Confirm component exports, props, and tokens
in the source before using them.

## Before writing UI

1. Find the closest existing page or component under `apps/frontend/src/`
   and follow its structure.
2. Map each element to an existing `@repo/theme` component.
3. Map each color and text style to an existing CSS variable.
4. Add a new component only when no existing one can express the element.

## Components

Import shared components from `@repo/theme`. Check the component index for
the current exports.

Components to look for include:

- Actions and input: `Button`, `IconButton`, `Input`, `Select`, `Checkbox`,
  `Switch`, `Slider`, `ColorPicker`, `DaySelect`
- Text: `Text`, `Heading`, `Label`
- Surfaces: `Card`, `Dialog`, `DropdownMenu`, `Accordion`, `Tooltip`,
  `HoverCard`, `Badge`, `PillSwitcher`
- Feedback: `Skeleton`, `LoadingIndicator`, `Boundary`
- Layout: `Box`, `Flex`, `Grid`, `Section`, `Container`

Check the implementation for supported props. Existing Button options
include `primary`, `secondary`, and `tertiary` variants, plus `noFill`
and `isDelete`. Text sizes are `"1"` through `"9"` and weights are
`regular`, `medium`, and `bold`.

- Generic primitives go in `packages/theme/src/components/`.
- Berkeleytime-specific composites, such as a class card, go in
  `apps/frontend/src/components/`.
- Page-specific views stay in `apps/frontend/src/app/`.

## Tokens

Token source:
`packages/theme/src/components/ThemeProvider/ThemeProvider.scss`.

Use semantic variables so light and dark mode stay in sync:

- `--heading-color`, `--paragraph-color`, `--description-color`,
  `--secondary-color`, `--label-color`
- `--background-color`, `--foreground-color`, `--backdrop-color`,
  `--border-color`
- `--heading-large`, `--heading-medium`, `--heading-small`,
  `--heading-caps`, `--body-medium`
- `--text-12`, `--text-14`, `--text-16`, `--text-18`, `--text-28`

`--heading-color` and `--paragraph-color` are text colors.
`--foreground-color` is a surface color, used for cards and tooltips,
not for text. `--background-color` is a background surface.

Use a palette variable such as `--blue-500` only when no semantic token
covers the case. Write `var(--token)` in SCSS. Leave raw hex and `rgb()`
out of component styles.

The theme does not define spacing, border-radius, or shadow tokens.
Define `margin`, `padding`, `gap`, radius, and shadow in the component
SCSS module, following nearby components. Do not assume Bootstrap
utilities are available.

## Styling

- Follow the existing SCSS module convention: `index.tsx` plus
  `[Component].module.scss`.
- Import styles with `import styles from "./Component.module.scss"`.
- Combine classes with `classNames` from `classnames`.
- Check the resulting UI in light and dark mode and at relevant
  viewport sizes.

## Figma

When a Figma URL or frame is provided, implement it with the existing
components and tokens. Treat the frame as the layout reference.
Keep Berkeleytime components, type scale, and colors.

Translate Figma Dev Mode variable names into tokens that exist in
`ThemeProvider.scss`. Confirm the token exists before using it.
```

## `.cursor/skills/backend-reliability/SKILL.md`

```markdown
---
name: backend-reliability
description: >-
  Make careful Berkeleytime backend changes that follow the existing module
  pipeline and persisted GraphQL gateway. Use when editing apps/backend,
  GraphQL schemas, resolvers, controllers, Mongoose models, Redis, Docker, or
  when the user mentions the API, auth, or a backend bug.
---

# Berkeleytime backend reliability

Follow the nearest relevant module and keep changes within the task.
Verify behavior and authorization as well as compilation. Do not copy
an existing pattern when it would allow unauthorized access.

## Before writing

1. Open the closest module under `apps/backend/src/modules/` and follow
   its structure.
2. Identify the files the task requires changing.
3. Determine whether the operation handles public catalog data,
   private user-owned data, or staff-only functionality.
4. Identify the authentication and authorization checks it needs.

## Module pipeline

modules/[domain]/
  ├── typedefs/
  ├── generated-types/    # generated; do not hand-edit
  ├── controller.ts
  ├── formatter.ts        # when the DB model and GraphQL type differ
  ├── resolver.ts
  └── index.ts

Request → Resolver → Controller → Database → Formatter → Response.

- Schema definitions for a module live in its `typedefs/`.
  Shared schema lives in `packages/gql-typedefs`.
- Mongoose models live in `packages/common/src/models/`.
- After a backend schema change, run `npm run generate` from
  `apps/backend`.
- Export a new module from `apps/backend/src/modules/index.ts`.

## GraphQL boundary

`/api/graphql` accepts a generated persisted-operation ID and variables.
The server owns the document.

- Leave raw Apollo HTTP middleware unmounted.
- Frontend operations live in `lib/api/` and use the generated
  `...Document` with `useQuery` or `useMutation`.
- Do not hand-edit `generated-types/`, `generated-typedefs/`, or
  `generated/`. Update source definitions and run the generator.

## Authentication and authorization

- Passport.js creates the session.
- `@auth` only rejects a request when
  `contextValue.user.isAuthenticated` is false. It does not compare
  record ownership or check a staff role.
- Put `@auth` on fields and mutations that require a signed-in user.
- Public catalog reads stay public, matching the nearest existing field.

### User-owned operations

- User-owned writes also require a controller-level authorization
  predicate that scopes the target record to the caller.
- Follow the ownership predicate used by the relevant model.
  For example, `deleteCollection` scopes access with
  `createdBy: context.user._id`.
- Include ownership in the database mutation filter where appropriate.
  Authentication plus a caller-supplied record ID is insufficient.
- Derive the caller identity from the trusted context, not from
  user-supplied owner fields.
- Protect private user-owned reads with the corresponding ownership
  or access check.
- Preserve intentional sharing or delegated access through the existing
  authorization policy.
- Follow the nearby controller's `NOT_FOUND` or `FORBIDDEN` behavior
  when access is denied, avoiding disclosure of private record existence.

### Staff-only operations

- Require the existing staff authorization check in addition to
  authentication.
- Follow the nearest staff-protected controller and use `FORBIDDEN`
  for insufficient permissions.

## GraphQL errors

- Throw `GraphQLError` with the code the nearest relevant controller
  already uses.
- Existing codes include:
  - `UNAUTHENTICATED`
  - `FORBIDDEN`
  - `BAD_USER_INPUT`
  - `INVALID_ARGUMENT`
  - `NOT_FOUND`
  - `CONFLICT`
  - `INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR`
- Keep `CONFLICT` for conflict cases, including the existing banner
  conflict behavior.
- Keep `INVALID_ARGUMENT` where the API uses it, including invalid
  rating metrics.
- Do not relabel these cases as `BAD_USER_INPUT` merely to standardize
  the code list.
- Return expected failures as GraphQL errors.
- Let unexpected failures surface. Do not add empty `catch` blocks
  that hide them.

## Data access

- Match the query bounds, projections, and indexes of the neighboring
  controller.
- Follow an existing Redis call site before adding a cache.
- Read UC Berkeley APIs through `@repo/sis-api`.
- Read environment variables from the existing `.env` pattern.

## Verification

- Run the checks relevant to the changed module.
- For authorization changes, verify that an unauthenticated caller is
  rejected, an authorized owner succeeds, and a different signed-in
  user cannot read private data or modify the owner's record by ID.
- For staff-only changes, verify that an ordinary signed-in user is
  rejected and an authorized staff user succeeds.
- Verify that public catalog reads remain public where intended.
- For changed error paths, verify that the existing error codes are
  preserved.
```

## `.cursor/rules/change-discipline.mdc`

```markdown
---
description: Keep Berkeleytime edits small, consistent with nearby code, and limited to the task
alwaysApply: true
---

# Change discipline

- Search the nearest module, page, or component and follow its relevant
  patterns.
- Edit only the files the task requires.
- Add a dependency, abstraction, or refactor only when the task requires it.
- If the requested behavior or authorization requirements conflict with
  an existing pattern, explain the conflict before copying it.
- UI uses existing `@repo/theme` components and variables from
  `ThemeProvider.scss`.
- Define spacing, radius, and shadows in component SCSS where the theme
  has no corresponding tokens.
- Backend stays on the persisted-operation gateway at `/api/graphql`.
- Do not hand-edit generated files; update their sources and regenerate.
- `@auth` checks authentication only. User-owned operations require
  controller-level ownership or access checks, and staff-only operations
  require staff authorization.
- Preserve the nearest relevant controller's GraphQL error semantics,
  including `CONFLICT` and `INVALID_ARGUMENT`.
- Verify the behavior affected by the change.
``

